{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"057256e0dcbcc97d","name":"card_unit_bt_7_1937","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Soviet/BrothersInArms/units/card_unit_bt_7_1937","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"BT-7 1937","text":"Becomes Veteran at the start of its fourth turn on the battlefield.","flavorText":"The BT-7 1937 was a Soviet light cavalry tank that was used during WWII. It was an improved version of the BT series of tanks, which were known for their speed and mobility. The BT-7 1937 featured a new turret and sloped armor, providing more protection compared to its predecessors. It had a crew of three and was armed with a 45 mm main gun, as well as two 7.62 mm DT machine guns. During the early stages of World War II, the BT-7 1937 played a role in the Soviet Union's defense against the German invasion. Its speed and agility made it effective in maneuvering on the battlefield. It could achieve speeds of up to 50 km/h in off-road conditions, allowing it to rapidly exploit enemy weaknesses and do hit-and-run tactics. However, the BT-7 1937 had its limitations. Its armor was relatively thin, making it vulnerable to enemy anti-tank fire. The tank's 45 mm gun was effective against most early-war German tanks, but became increasingly inadequate against more heavily armored adversaries as the war progressed. Despite its shortcomings, the BT-7 1937 was used throughout the war. It was primarily employed for reconnaissance, infantry support, and exploitation of breakthroughs. However, as newer and more advanced tanks became available, such as the T-34 and KV series, the BT-7 1937 was gradually phased out of frontline service.","faction":"Soviet","type":"tank","rarity":"Rare","cost":2,"operationCost":1,"attack":2,"defense":3},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_unit_bt_7_1937","title":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_unit_bt_7_1937_title","SourceString":"BT-7 1937","LocalizedString":"BT-7 1937"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::tank","attack":2,"de